@startuml sequence-mcp-write-gate
' Sequence: the MCP write path and its two authorisation gates, after ADR-0036.
'
' The read path is drawn in mcp-tool-call.puml. This one exists because the
' write path is the only place an agent changes the line, so its gates are
' worth a picture of their own.
'
' Grounded in real source:
'   - src/mcp/McpInitRoot.cpp   (agentSession_ + setSession unconditional,
'                                setAudit only when initialize() succeeds)
'   - src/mcp/tools/EquipmentCommandTool.cpp
'                               (currentUser gate, hasSession gate, invoke,
'                                allowed() re-check to shape the reply)
'   - src/presenter/DashboardPresenter.h   (setSession / setAudit / hasSession)
'   - src/presenter/DashboardPresenter.cpp (checkRole + emitAudit)

title MCP equipment_command - two gates before the model moves

skinparam shadowing false

participant "LLM agent" as Agent
participant "EquipmentCommandTool\n::runEquipmentCommand" as Tool
participant "DashboardPresenter" as Presenter
participant "AuditLogger\n(may be absent)" as Audit
participant "ProductionModel" as Model

== wiring, at boot (McpInitRoot) ==
note over Presenter
  setSession(agentSession) runs ALWAYS.
  setAudit(auditLogger, agentSession) runs
  only when the audit database opened.
  Before ADR-0036 the session came only from
  setAudit, so a failed open removed the gate.
end note

== gate 1: is there an agent at all ==
Agent -> Tool : equipment_command { command: "reset" }
Tool -> Tool : session.currentUser()
alt no authenticated agent
    Tool --> Agent : Unauthorized\n(refused BEFORE touching the presenter)
end

== gate 2: is the presenter's own gate wired ==
Tool -> Presenter : hasSession()
alt session pointer absent
    note right of Tool
      checkRole() passes a null session through
      by design, so routing the call here would
      perform an ungated write. Refuse instead
      of trusting the wiring (ADR-0036).
    end note
    Tool --> Agent : Unauthorized\n(model untouched)
end

== the call, through the same handler a human button uses ==
Tool -> Presenter : onResetRestartClicked()
Presenter -> Presenter : checkRole(audit_, session_, canResetSystem)
alt role not permitted
    Presenter -> Audit : emitAudit(..., FAILURE)
    note right of Audit : no-op when the sink is absent
    Presenter --> Tool : void (silent no-op)
else role permitted
    Presenter -> Audit : emitAudit(..., SUCCESS)
    Presenter -> Model : resetSystem()
    Presenter --> Tool : void
end

== the reply is shaped by re-evaluating the same predicate ==
Tool -> Tool : mapping.allowed(agent->role)
alt not permitted
    Tool --> Agent : Unauthorized\n(the refusal above is already audited)
else permitted
    Tool --> Agent : { command, status: "accepted" }
end

note over Tool, Model
  The presenter's gate returns void, so the tool cannot
  read its verdict. That is why the predicate is evaluated
  twice: once inside the presenter to act and audit, once
  in the tool to turn a silent no-op into a structured
  JSON-RPC error instead of a misleading success.
end note

@enduml
